        ":question/id": "a9e76cd0-6e63-52b6-ad92-c0e43ec54487",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-30270",
        ":question/problem": "For the curve $y=5({6}^{x})+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={6}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=5({6}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=5+2=7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5({6}^{0})+2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =5+2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=5({6}^{x})+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,7).$"
      },
