        ":question/id": "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-30269",
        ":question/problem": "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"
      },
